WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.457 --> 00:00:02.040
MARTIN OMANDER:
Wilden recently asked,

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.710
what if I want to access
my private Google Cloud

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.420
Storage from Cloud Run?

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.570
Good question.

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:09.120
It depends on what you need.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.120
One, your Cloud Run service
may need to access files

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.160
that belong to it.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.040
Two, your service may need to
access one particular Google

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.350
Doc, like a Google Sheet.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.980
Or three, your service may need
to access the current user's

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.090
Google Docs.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.440
In this video, let's
talk about the first one.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.860
The other two will
get separate videos.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.870
If you only need to
read files, and they

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.610
don't change very
often, you can simply

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.140
include them in the container.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.780
Your code can then read
them using the native file

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.400
reading API.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.890
If you need to both
read and write files,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.840
or if the files
change frequently,

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.700
you can create a
bucket in Cloud Storage

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.060
and use the storage client
library to access that bucket.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.820
If you're not using the default
service account for Cloud Run,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.770
just remember to give
the service account

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.840
access to the bucket.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.420
Stay tuned for how to access
Google Docs from Cloud Run.

